# Experiment 5 – FTP File Transfer and GitHub Push

I checked your uploaded expt 5.pdf. The experiment is titled:

Aim: Storage file accessed remotely (VM) using FTP (FileZilla, CuteFTP or WinSCP), configure the server, transfer files to the VM using FTP, initialize a Git repository and push it to GitHub.&#x20;

expt 5.pdf



Below are the commands based on the work we completed in Ubuntu, using your existing GitHub repository `hello_test`.

## Step 1: Install FTP and Git

Open Ubuntu Terminal.

```
sudo apt update
```

```
sudo apt install vsftpd ftp git -y
```

## Step 2: Start the FTP server

```
sudo systemctl start vsftpd
```

```
sudo systemctl enable vsftpd
```

Check the FTP server:

```
sudo systemctl status vsftpd
```

Press `q` to exit the status screen.

## Step 3: Create FTP user

```
sudo adduser ftpuser
```

Enter a password when prompted. Keep it private.

## Step 4: Create a folder and sample file

```
mkdir -p ~/experiment5
```

```
echo "This is my Experiment 5 FTP file transfer." > ~/experiment5/sample.txt
```

Set permissions:

```
sudo chown -R ftpuser:ftpuser /home/ftpuser/experiment5
```

If your folder was created under a different user's home directory, use its actual path for the ownership command.

## Step 5: Find your Ubuntu IP address

```
ip a
```

Your earlier IP address was:

```
10.0.2.15
```

## Step 6: Connect to FTP

```
ftp 10.0.2.15
```

Enter:

```
Name: ftpuser
Password: Your FTP password
```

Successful connection message:

```
230 Login successful.
ftp>
```

## Step 7: Transfer the file using FTP

Inside the `ftp>` prompt, run these commands one by one:

```
ls
```

```
cd experiment5
```

```
ls
```

Download the file:

```
get sample.txt
```

Exit FTP:

```
bye
```

FTP transfer completed

## Step 8: Create a local Git repository folder

Back in the normal Ubuntu terminal:

```
mkdir -p ~/experiment5-git
```

```
cd ~/experiment5-git
```

Copy the downloaded file into this folder:

```
cp /home/vboxuser/sample.txt .
```

Check the file:

```
ls
```

You should see:

```
sample.txt
```

## Step 9: Initialize the Git repository

```
git init
```

Configure your Git identity:

```
git config --global user.name "Vaibhavi"
```

```
git config --global user.email "YOUR_GITHUB_EMAIL"
```

Add the file:

```
git add .
```

Check the status:

```
git status
```

Commit the file:

```
git commit -m "Experiment 5 FTP file transfer"
```

Local Git commit completed

## Step 10: Connect your existing GitHub repository

Your existing repository:

`https://github.com/Vaibhavi-2406/hello_test.git`

Run:

```
git remote add origin https://github.com/Vaibhavi-2406/hello_test.git
```

Set the branch:

```
git branch -M main
```

Push to GitHub:

```
git push -u origin main
```

If GitHub asks for authentication, use your GitHub username and a Personal Access Token instead of your account password. Never share your token.

GitHub push completed

